WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.750
We left the story last time celebrating the 30th anniversary of the Macintosh. 
The thing that used to enrage Steve Jobs,

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.719
"It's wonderful Steve, it's cute, but it's a bit of a toy.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:17.760
What is it for?"
 And here's the answer, it was the Apple Laserwriter. I should apologize right at the outset.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.949
We tried everything we knew to get ahold of an original Apple Laserwriter I,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.930
but we just couldn't. This one is a fairly early 
Apple Laserwriter II.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.170
This one is dated 1988...

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:34.979
There's one behind me that we got off ebay.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.360
We might refer to that one a little bit later on. The one behind me is even earlier. It's 1987,

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.980
as opposed to 1985, but we did the best we could.

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:54.329
Steve Jobs had the foresight to invest very heavily in this other technology that came out of Xerox Parc.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.340
John Warnock, Chuck Geschke, founding Adobe and having this new

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:04.860
two-dimensional graphics language that was also suitable for being a page description language,

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.269
for laser printers and typesetters, it was called Postscript.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.139
Jobs invested very heavily in this. He bought Adobe stock,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:20.699
and I think John Warnock told me he gave them a million and a half dollars in upfront royalties because, again,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.389
Jobs is farsighted, and he got it right. He thought that

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:31.169
the combination of the Mac with the Laserwriter would be just magic. They couldn't get it to market

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:36.089
in time for the launch of the Mac in '84, it had to wait a whole year. So we're talking early 1985.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:42.509
Despite locating these two laser printers, the thing that causes problems with these things

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.769
when they get as old as these are is the power supply.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:54.989
It's not that the power supply doesn't work, it does. But the one part of a laser printer cycle which is really demanding

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:02.850
is not so much running the logic inside it to typeset the page. It's the fact that when you roll out the page,

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.430
you have to, in the end,

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:08.488
fuse the toner ink,

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:14.919
with heat, onto the paper to make it stick permanently. And, to cut a long story short,

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:20.019
the power supplies on this one and on the one behind me are now so tired,

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:26.889
they just sulk furiously if they're asked to give up seven amps just like that. But I will turn it on,

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:32.409
and hopefully shall now be able to zoom in and show you what happens. The green lights

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:39.548
flash as it's booting up, you end up full of hope... The green light on the left will start blinking. It's just checking,

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:44.649
that's all though. Its logic board here is quite happy, Postscript fonts begin [unintelligible]. Oh dear.

00:02:45.829 --> 00:02:50.858
It's blinking red, and we've tried everything we know. 
So, sorry we can't actually

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.409
run the thing for you, however, when it was working and wasn't sulking...

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:01.599
This is your boot-up sheet, and it is just a playful thing,

00:03:02.329 --> 00:03:08.409
done with proper fonts and full computer graphics. 
You can probably see at the top that

00:03:09.079 --> 00:03:12.608
there's a capital "P". They told you that it's Postscript.

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:19.029
There is much use down here, a Sans-Serif font, and those of you who know about these things will say yes, it is Helvetica.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:21.790
Reporting even that there is

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.250
RS-232, a serial line,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:31.599
RS-422. Now that's an adaptation of the ISO standard that, in the Apple domain, was called "Appletalk".

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.340
It's a medium speed network. It's better than serial line.

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:39.549
Appletalk could do 230 kilobits.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.239
230 kilobits is okay, but it's not Ethernet.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:47.649
Early Ethernet is 10 megabits, way faster again.

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:55.089
So you've got a network of sorts, but not a very high-speed one. It's telling you how many fonts there are inside it.

00:03:55.209 --> 00:04:01.598
It's got 35 built-in fonts. The Laserwriter I only had 13, and they're down at the bottom.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:07.389
It's telling you that this particular one is equipped with two megabytes of RAM. Now,

00:04:07.389 --> 00:04:10.299
that was a lot in those days. And it wasn't

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:14.229
there to support a multitasking game-playing machine,

00:04:14.229 --> 00:04:20.049
it was there simply and solely to support font rendering to the laser printer drum.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.390
And the chip inside that Laserwriter, as we'll see later on,

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.679
was of the 68000 family,

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.240
just like it is in the Macintosh, but run at a much faster

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.479
clock speed. By the time it got to this laser printer
 over here,

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.439
it was faster still. It was a 68020, and boy,

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.380
did you need it. You really needed that to get it to work at all. A problem with a laser printer

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:53.230
is that if you've got your page all bitmapped and rendered and ready to go and,

00:04:54.050 --> 00:05:00.909
you know, an A4 or US letter size page, you're talking about a megabyte, you're talking 8 megabits' worth of

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.430
black and white dots.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:09.460
But once inside here, you tell the drum to start imaging stuff on the paper,

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:16.749
and you start rolling the paper out. You are committed. You can't say "Stop, I want to do the bottom half a bit later,

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.760
I'm running out of out of compute power". You can't do that. When you got to go,

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.840
you got to go. Some of you have seen the previous videos on the

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:32.229
202 Typesetter, can sort of draw the contrast here. The 202 had the great advantage that it was all done in

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:35.379
letterbox strips, the imaging onto a reel of paper.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:40.119
And if you, after you turn one letterbox full, you wanted to go away

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.920
and have a cup of tea before you did the rest, well then, you could. There's no problem. But not with this.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.400
It's utterly page-based. When you roll, you've got to roll,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:55.630
you can't stop. And it was a huge demand to be able to keep up with the roll-out speed of this.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:58.719
And believe me, it was only just doable,

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.650
particularly with Laserwriter I technology.

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:10.719
There were 13 fonts inside the earlier one. When I say fonts, you know, what would the 13 have been? Four flavours of Times Roman,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:15.580
regular, bold, bold italic, and italic, four flavours of Helvetica,

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:21.369
ordinary, oblique, so on... four flavours of Courier, a fixed-pitch font,

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:29.170
bold, bold oblique, ordinary and so on, and one mathematical symbols font. Three 4s are 12, plus 1 is 13.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:34.210
And they're built in, as we shall see. Steve Jobs, who had done a

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:39.879
graphics arts degree at Reed College, I think, in Oregon, although he dropped out before he got his degree,

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.320
knew all about

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.080
typefaces, their names, all this kind of stuff. And he said "John, John," you know, "We've got to save money.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.949
Can't we just go with with clone fonts, you know, things that look like Times Roman

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:55.990
or San Serif, Helvetica?" And John Tommy said,        "Look Steve,

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:58.780
I hope I've got this the right way around?

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:03.520
You drink Coke don't you? How do you feel Steve, if I offer you a can of Pepsi?"

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:09.340
And the look of horror on Jobs' face... and John said, 
"I think I made my point, Steve.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:17.139
If we're going to position this as the serious future of typesetting, we've got to have proper fonts that 
people have

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:20.620
heard of." So he won the day, and I think he was right.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:26.139
He largely did help to convince the typesetting community that this thing,

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:30.730
although it had problems at the beginning, 
was on the road to

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:37.059
revolutionising typesetting. Well, I've taken out the logic board from the Laserwriter II

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:40.269
that is behind me, and this one is dated

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.420
1987. And it's a real eye-opener.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:48.520
It was really taking me back. Here, on my left, your right, is the RAM,

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:54.340
two megabytes' worth of it. The Laserwriter I had one-and-a-half, but they found out pretty quickly

00:07:54.340 --> 00:08:01.359
they ideally needed more. This is soldered onto the board. All of these bits make up your two megabytes here.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:08.409
Here, one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight... 
Eight ROMs.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.400
And, at the time that came out in the Laserwriter I, those eight ROMs,

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:19.360
constituting half a megabyte of firmware inside here, was a world record. No prizes for guessing

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.710
that in here is the Postscript interpreter, a minimal little

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:29.559
operating system that could read stuff down the serial line or access extra formats from disk.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:33.100
So there's a Postscript interpreter is in here, plus

00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:37.749
embedded at this stage, the 35 fonts, rather than the 13.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.760
Well, there's a CPU chip. This one, being the 1987 model, is still a

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:49.039
Motorola 68000, 16-bit. If we look inside that one, being a little bit younger and later,

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:56.270
it's moved on to being a 68020 at that stage. 
Over here is the much-vaunted Appletalk.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:02.090
Over at this side is the two possible sockets that gave you the option for serial line,

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:08.599
that would run a standard up to 9.6 kilobits, but I think there was a switch setting here

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:11.540
you could do to make it run at 19.2.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:20.869
Obviously, if you're doing serious work with this, you use the Appletalk whenever you could. Steve Jobs is very happy with it running over Appletalk,

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.190
preferably with just a roomful of Macs
 all sharing the Appletalk.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.470
But those of us who bought one of these things for 7,000 pounds were not really happy

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:34.190
unless it could be used with other computers.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:38.510
So there was a big growth industry shortly after this was released, in

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:44.929
linking Ethernet networking to Appletalk, with names like "Spider Box" to enable you to do that.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:49.460
So, yes, it eventually became shareable. 
You can justify the huge

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.090
expenditure in buying the thing. 
So here we are in early 1985,

